  "documentation": {
    "range": "This widespread species occurs almost throughout Europe (Bank 2017). Whilst the original range is uncertain, Wiktor (1996, 2001) believed that it was probably southern and western Europe (Wiktor 1996, 2001) whereas Castillejo (1997) speculated that it was from central Europe. Whatever the original range was, it has since spread as a synanthrope throughout the region. It extends into European Russia (Sysoev and Schileyko 2009) and has been introduced to temperate regions of all continents except Antarctica.<strong></strong>",
    "population": "There are no total population size data for this species and they cannot reliably be estimated. However, it can be one of the most common slug species present at a site. The population size is therefore assumed to be relatively stable. It may even be increasing as the species is introduced to new areas.",
    "habitats": "This omnivorous species is highly tolerant of human disturbance and occurs in a wide range of sheltered habitats, including artificial ones around buildings, and was perhaps originally a forest species (Wiktor 1996, 2001, Welter-Schultes 2012, Rowson <em>et al.</em> 2014). It reaches 1,500 m altitude in Bulgaria and 1,900 m in Switzerland (Welter-Schultes 2012).",
    "threats": "This species is not considered threatened at a European level, nor at any national level.",
    "measures": "There are no especial conservation measures for this species and it does not appear on any national red lists.",
    "use_trade": "This species is not used or traded.",
    "rationale": "Global and European regional assessment: Least Concern (LC)<br/>EU 28 regional assessment: Least Concern (LC)<br/><br/>This species is very widespread and often common throughout Europe. Its original range is uncertain, but was probably southern and western Europe, or central Europe. It has since spread as a synanthrope. Beyond Europe it has been introduced to temperate regions of all continents except Antarctica. There are no major threats affecting the whole population of the species. It is therefore listed as Least Concern (LC).",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": "This species is vulnerable to confusion with some other, less well-known <em>Limax </em>species in southern Europe."
  },
